Hi folks,

Very helpful suggestions regarding treating sex==NA to sex==Unknown
But even with these changes, the gl.sexlinkage() is still not working for me and is throwing the following error:
Error in a[, 2] : subscript out of bounds

After following Rujiporn's suggestion on dropping the sex==Unknown, I get the following warning and same error:
gl.sexlinkage(ld_FM)
Starting gl.sexlinkage
  Warning: genlight object contains monomorphic loci
Error in a[, 2] : subscript out of bounds

After filtering out monomorphs
I still get the same error: Error in a[, 2] : subscript out of bounds

Any advice? I will try to test the function line by line to trace back to what is throwing the error.

Another question is regarding workflow. What filters should I perform (CallRate ,AvgCount etc) PRIOR to estimating sex linked loci? Your expertise would be super appreciated!

Best,

Fonti (Noble Lab - ANU, UNSW)

On Friday, 10 May 2019 at 18:22:06 UTC+10 Rujiporn Sun wrote:
> Hi Sarah,
>
> I have just tried the script with my data too. I removed NA from the data and everything works fine. Here is the script.
>
> #Check column name to make sure there is a sex column in ind.metrics
> names(gl@other$ind.metrics)
>
> #Subset samples with only F and M
> pop(gl) <- gl@other$ind.metrics$sex
> gl_s <- gl.keep.pop(gl,pop.list=c("F","M"))
>
> #Filter potential sex-linked loci
> gl.sexlinkage(gl_s)
>
> Hope this helps.
>
> R.
>

